From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ram: no pin on a corner track, address pins spill to the
 bottom edge
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

generate_ram placed its pins with no corner avoidance, and put every
address pin on the right edge. A pin on the first track along an edge
is a track from the perpendicular edge, and a via from it to the layer
below lands its enclosure outside the die (asap7: VIA34's M3 reaches
68 nm below the first M4 track, which is 48 nm up): detailed routing
found no path (DRT-0255) for a block of 16 words with two read and four
write ports, whose read address pin took that track. A block of 8 such
words has 18 address pins for a 2.43 um right edge, and pin placement
stopped (PPL-0111).

No pin centre is now placed within a pitch of a die corner, and the
address pins take the right edge only for as many ports as a third of
its free tracks hold (the pin placer gave 13 slots for the 8-word
block's 32 free tracks); the other ports' take the bottom edge.

pins_corner_asap7 places the 16-word block's pins as generate_ram does
and checks no signal pin's centre lies within a pitch of a corner;
without this change addr_r0[0] does. routed_short_edge_asap7 routes the
8-word block with each write port writing its own words, checks some
address pins went to the bottom edge, and reads back 300 random cycles;
without this change it stops at PPL-0111. The 16-word block routes too,
but its detailed route takes over 20 minutes on asap7, too slow for a
test.

Two Nangate45 golden blocks move a pin off a corner track (make_7x7
and make_7x7_2r1w: one pin 0.56 um along its edge, the slot count and I/O HPWL
in their logs); their .defok, .lefok and .ok follow.

Carried in bazel-orfs, not upstreamed; retires with the //:bump onto an
OpenROAD that has it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
